     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change my concern, and I shouldn't have suggested earlier that I could enter it with a note.\n\nA dose of 16,000 mg/day is about five times the maximum daily dose of ibuprofen. No indication I know of calls for it, and it could seriously harm the patient. The risks include GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, and CNS effects. Normal kidney and liver function and no allergies don't offset that.\n\nHere is what I'd suggest:\n1. **Check the intended dose.** 400 mg four times daily is a common typo-level mistake, and it gives 1600 mg/day.\n2. **Talk to your resident or attending.** If they want an unusual dose, they should enter it themselves or co-sign it directly. Pharmacy will probably want to review it too.\n3. **Tell me the dose you want.** I can enter any dose within the standard range right away, for example 400 mg, 600 mg, or 800 mg by mouth with the frequency you choose."
      }
     ],
